# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. The Airtable PAT belongs in the Netlify UI only -- never in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env
secrets.json
*.pem
*.key
airtable_pat*
pat.txt

# Netlify
.netlify/
.netlify-cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Azure Static Web Apps
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api/_netlify/ is generated by dev/build-azure-api.js: a verbatim copy of
# netlify/{functions,lib} staged into the deploy package, because SWA ships
# api/ and nothing above it. Committing it would put a second copy of the auth
# boundary in the tree, which is the drift the adapter exists to avoid --
# `node dev/build-azure-api.js --check` fails the build if the copy is stale,
# and CI runs it before every deploy.
api/_netlify/

# local.settings.json is where `func start` reads AIRTABLE_PAT and
# SESSION_SECRET. Same rule as .env: it holds real credentials and never goes
# in the repo. Use api/local.settings.json.example as the template.
local.settings.json
api/local.settings.json

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
package-lock.json.bak

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Local dev/test harnesses.
#
# dev/ was excluded entirely until 2026-08. "Never deploy or commit" conflated
# two different things: it must never be SERVED, which publish = "public" in
# netlify.toml already guarantees on its own (plus a forced 404 on /dev/*), and
# never be COMMITTED, which cost the repo its own build tooling. The result was a
# README documenting `node dev/build-live-pages.js` against a directory the repo
# did not contain, and the script that produces every published page living on
# one laptop.
#
# It matters more now: dev/seed-admin.js is the only way to bootstrap the first
# admin account, and dev/verify-auth.sh is the test suite for the auth boundary.
# Losing either is not recoverable from the repo.
#
# Nothing in dev/ holds a credential — the scripts read AIRTABLE_PAT from the
# environment. Keep it that way; the .env rules at the top of this file still
# apply, and a token must never be pasted into a script.
dev/**/*.env
dev/**/secrets*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Design-tool exports and data snapshots. The production build (index.html) is
# generated from these; the sources themselves are large, contain real
# homesite data, and do not belong in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.zip
*.dc.html
olh-data.js
completion-data.js
support.js
uploads/
production/
.thumbnail

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Staged design exports (dev/release.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# exports/<date>/ is where release.sh puts each export: raw/ verbatim, pages/
# renamed to deployed names. Same reason as the rules above -- olh-data.js is
# ~2 MB of real homesite records, and the .dc.html templates are large. They are
# build inputs, not source we maintain.
#
# exports/manifest.json IS committed, and is the whole point of the arrangement:
# without it, a design change is a 1 MB diff in a generated file with no way to
# tell which export produced it. The manifest is names, sizes and content hashes
# -- diffable, no data in it, and enough to answer "which export is prod on?"
exports/*
!exports/manifest.json
